Recompiling libgeos for the browser can be quite a task, but using Emscripten can make the process easier. Emscripten is an LLVM-based project that converts C/C++ code into WebAssembly or asm.js to run in browsers.

However, instead of doing everything from scratch, I recommend considering using a prebuilt library that already provides the required bindings. One such library is `geos-wasm`. This project compiles libgeos to WebAssembly and exposes the needed functionality in a more user-friendly, JavaScript API.

You can find the geos-wasm GitHub repository here:
https://github.com/hugovk/geos-wasm

You can add `geos-wasm` as a dependency to your project using npm:

```bash
npm install geos-wasm
```

After installing the package, you can import it into your project and start using the needed functionalities:

```javascript
import GEOSS from "geos-wasm";

GEOSS().then((geos) => {
  // Load WKT
  const reader = new geos.WKTReader();
  const geometry = reader.read("POLYGON((0 0, 10 0, 10 10, 0 10, 0 0))");

  // Perform operations
  const buffered = geometry.buffer(5);
  const intersection = geometry.intersection(buffered);
  const isValid = geometry.isValid();

  // Results
  console.log("Buffered:", buffered.toWKT());
  console.log("Intersection:", intersection.toWKT());
  console.log("Is valid:", isValid);

  // Clean up
  geos.destroy(reader);
  geos.destroy(geometry);
  geos.destroy(buffered);
  geos.destroy(intersection);
});
```

Using `geos-wasm` will save you time and effort since it already provides the functionalities from libgeos that you need, in a browser-compatible package.

To bind a React Portal inside another component, you can follow these steps:

1. First, import the necessary modules from React:

```javascript
import React, { Component } from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
```

2. Create a new component for your Portal. This component will render its children inside a specified DOM element (in this case, a new `div`):

```javascript
class MyPortal extends Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.el = document.createElement('div');
  }

  componentDidMount() {
    document.body.appendChild(this.el);
  }

  componentWillUnmount() {
    document.body.removeChild(this.el);
  }

  render() {
    return ReactDOM.createPortal(
      this.props.children,
      this.el
    );
  }
}
```

3. Now, you can use the `MyPortal` component inside any other component and the content will be rendered inside the specified DOM element:

```javascript
class App extends Component {
  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <h1>Main App Component</h1>
        <MyPortal>
          <h2>Content inside the Portal component</h2>
        </MyPortal>
      </div>
    );
  }
}
```

In this example, we've created a `MyPortal` component that renders its children inside a new `div` element, which is then appended to the document body. By using this `MyPortal` component within the `App` component, we can render content from the "App" component inside a different DOM element, effectively binding the React Portal inside another component.

