You can use the `Process` class in C# to run another command-line program and interact with it. Here is an example of how you can do this:
```
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Diagnostics;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Start the other command-line program
        Process process = new Process();
        process.StartInfo = new ProcessStartInfo
        {
            FileName = "cmd.exe",
            Arguments = "/C echo Hello, world!",
            UseShellExecute = false,
            RedirectStandardInput = true,
            RedirectStandardOutput = true
        };
        process.Start();

        // Send input to the other command-line program
        process.StandardInput.WriteLine("This is some input.");

        // Read the output from the other command-line program
        string output = process.StandardOutput.ReadToEnd();
        Console.WriteLine(output);

        // Wait for the other command-line program to exit
        process.WaitForExit();
    }
}
```
In this example, we start a new instance of the "cmd.exe" program and pass the command "/C echo Hello, world!" as its arguments. This will cause the new command-line program to print "Hello, world!" to its output.

We then set the `RedirectStandardInput` property of the `ProcessStartInfo` object to `true`, which allows us to send input to the new command-line program using its `StandardInput` property. In this example, we send the string "This is some input." to the new command-line program using its `WriteLine` method.

Finally, we read the output from the new command-line program using its `StandardOutput` property, and print it to the console. We then wait for the new command-line program to exit using the `WaitForExit` method of the `Process` object.

