```
After that, you can reference these environment variables in the steps. This will eliminate the need to duplicate the variables in each step. For example, use `RAILS_ENV`, `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, `POSTGRES_USER`, and the other environment variables instead of repeating the entire `env` section in each step.

With this approach, your `yml` file will be cleaner and easier to maintain, and you'll avoid having duplicate environment variables.

Using the `@google-cloud/firestore` package is recommended for interacting with Firestore with TypeScript. The `firestore-admin` package may not be the best fit for this task. In this response, I am providing an example using the `@google-cloud/firestore` package which is the official package for Firestore.

First, make sure you have installed the necessary packages:

```bash
npm install --save @google-cloud/firestore
npm install --save @types/google-cloud__firestore
npm install --save typescript
```

To use Firestore in your TypeScript project, follow these steps:

1. Import the relevant classes:

```typescript
import { Firestore } from '@google-cloud/firestore';
```

2. Initialize a Firestore instance:

```typescript
const firestore = new Firestore({
  projectId: 'your-project-id',
  keyFilename: 'path-to-service-account-json-file', // Optional, if you don't use application default credentials
});
```

3. Define an interface for your document's structure:

```typescript
interface MyDocument {
  name: string;
  age: number;
  city: string;
}
```

4. Now you can perform CRUD operations with Firestore:

Create a document:

```typescript
async function createDocument() {
  const myDoc: MyDocument = {
    name: 'John Doe',
    age: 30,
    city: 'San Francisco',
  };

  const documentRef = firestore.collection('my-collection').doc('my-document');
  await documentRef.set(myDoc);

  console.log(`Document created successfully`);
}
```

Read a document:

```typescript
async function readDocument() {
  const documentRef = firestore.collection('my-collection').doc('my-document');
  const documentSnapshot = await documentRef.get();

  if (documentSnapshot.exists) {
    const myDoc = documentSnapshot.data() as MyDocument;
    console.log('Document data:', myDoc);
  } else {
    console.log('No document found');
  }
}
```

Update a document:

```typescript
async function updateDocument() {
  const documentRef = firestore.collection('my-collection').doc('my-document');
  await documentRef.update({ age: 31 });

  console.log('Document updated successfully');
}
```

Delete a document:

```typescript
async function deleteDocument() {
  const documentRef = firestore.collection('my-collection').doc('my-document');
  await documentRef.delete();

  console.log('Document deleted successfully');
}
```

Don't forget to call the functions to execute the corresponding operations. With this setup, you can use TypeScript to handle Firestore data and utilize type checking.

Visual Studio 2010 is a popular integrated development environment (IDE) used for developing software applications. To determine the larger of two numbers input on the screen, you can write a C# console application using Visual Studio 2010. Here's a step-by-step process to write and run the application:

