To build an Electron JS app with an SOS alert feature to send messages to WhatsApp, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. Install Node.js and Electron:

If you haven't already, install Node.js from https://nodejs.org/. After installing Node.js, use the following commands to install Electron globally:

```sh
npm install -g electron
```

2. Create a new project:

Create a new project directory, and add an empty `package.json` file in it:

```sh
mkdir electron-sos
cd electron-sos
npm init -y
```

3. Install required dependencies:

We will use the `electron-builder` package for building the application, `axios` for making API calls, and `open` to open WhatsApp web.

```sh
npm install electron-builder axios open
```

4. Create index.html and main.js files:

Create a new `index.html` file in the project directory and add the following content:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <title>SOS Alert App</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <button id="sendAlert">Send SOS Alert</button>

    <script>
      document.getElementById("sendAlert").addEventListener("click", () => {
        window.api.send("sendSOS", "Help, I need assistance!");
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

Create a new `main.js` file and add the following content:

```javascript
const { app, BrowserWindow, ipcMain } = require("electron");
const axios = require("axios");
const open = require("open");

function createWindow() {
  const win = new BrowserWindow({
    width: 800,
    height: 600,
    webPreferences: {
      nodeIntegration: false,
      contextIsolation: true,
      preload: __dirname + "/preload.js",
    },
  });

  win.loadFile("index.html");
}

app.whenReady().then(createWindow);

app.on("window-all-closed", () => {
  if (process.platform !== "darwin") {
    app.quit();
  }
});

app.on("activate", () => {
  if (BrowserWindow.getAllWindows().length === 0) {
    createWindow();
  }
});

ipcMain.handle("sendSOS", async (_, message) => {
  const phoneNumber = '1234567890'; // Replace with the desired phone number
  const encodedMessage = encodeURIComponent(message);
  const apiURL = `https://api.callmebot.com/whatsapp.php?phone=${phoneNumber}&text=${encodedMessage}&apikey=`;

  try {
    const response = await axios.get(apiURL);
    console.log(response.data);
    if (response.data.includes("WhatsApp Message Sent")) {
      open(`https://web.whatsapp.com/send?phone=${phoneNumber}&text=${encodedMessage}`);
    }
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
  }
});
```

5. Create preload.js:

Create a `preload.js` file in the project directory and add the following content:

```javascript
const { contextBridge, ipcRenderer } = require("electron");

contextBridge.exposeInMainWorld("api", {
  send: (channel, data) => {
    ipcRenderer.send(channel, data);
  },
});
```

6. Modify package.json:

Update the `package.json` file to include the main entry point (`main.js`) and scripts for starting and building the app:

```json
{
  "name": "electron-sos",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "",
  "main": "main.js",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "electron .",
    "build": "electron-builder"
  },
  "keywords": [],
  "author": "",
  "license": "ISC",
  "devDependencies": {
    "electron": "^13.1.6",
    "electron-builder": "^22.11.9"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "axios": "^0.21.1",
    "open": "^8.3.0"
  }
}
```

7. Run the app:

Use the following command to start the Electron app:

```sh
npm start
```

8. Build the app:

To generate a distributable package for your Electron app, run:

```sh
npm run build
```

_Note:_ When using the SOS feature, replace the `phoneNumber` in `main.js` with the recipient's number, including the country code (e.g., 11234567890).

Keep in mind that this solution uses CallMeBot API, which has certain limitations and is not an official WhatsApp API, so using it for commercial or production purposes is not recommended.

