2. Add a new custom function node, "scrape-providers".
3. Implement the custom function with an async function, `scrapeProviderRecursive`.
4. Define a new async function, `fetchData`, inside the custom function.
5. Call the "scrape-provider" node with the input parameters.
6. Check if there is a next page, and if so, call `fetchData` recursively with the new 'from' value.

This revised config extracts the pagination logic into a custom function, making it more readable and maintainable.

To obtain cluster, node, and pod information using C# code, you can use the Kubernetes .NET client, which is available on NuGet as the `KubernetesClient` package. Here's a step-by-step guide to do this:

1. Install the `KubernetesClient` NuGet package in your project

```
dotnet add package KubernetesClient
```

2. Add the necessary namespaces in your C# code:

```csharp
using k8s;
using k8s.Models;
```

3. Configure and create a Kubernetes client:

```csharp
KubernetesClientConfiguration config = KubernetesClientConfiguration.InClusterConfig();
IKubernetes client = new Kubernetes(config);
```

4. Query the Kubernetes API to get the information you need:

To get node information:

```csharp
var nodeList = await client.ListNodeAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
foreach (var node in nodeList.Items)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Node Name: {node.Metadata.Name}");
}
```

To get pod information:

```csharp
var podList = await client.ListNamespacedPodAsync("default").ConfigureAwait(false);
foreach (var pod in podList.Items)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Pod Name: {pod.Metadata.Name} | Namespace: {pod.Metadata.Namespace}");
}
```

These examples assume that your application is running inside a Kubernetes cluster. If you're running your application outside the cluster, you'll need to use a different configuration like `KubernetesClientConfiguration.BuildConfigFromConfigFile()` and provide the appropriate configuration file.

Here's a complete example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using k8s;
using k8s.Models;

namespace KubernetesExample
{
    class Program
    {
        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            KubernetesClientConfiguration config = KubernetesClientConfiguration.InClusterConfig();
            IKubernetes client = new Kubernetes(config);

            Console.WriteLine("Node information:");
            var nodeList = await client.ListNodeAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
            foreach (var node in nodeList.Items)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"- Node Name: {node.Metadata.Name}");
            }

            Console.WriteLine("\nPod information:");
            var podList = await client.ListNamespacedPodAsync("default").ConfigureAwait(false);
            foreach (var pod in podList.Items)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"- Pod Name: {pod.Metadata.Name} | Namespace: {pod.Metadata.Namespace}");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code will print out the names of nodes and pods in the default namespace. You can modify the queries to obtain additional information that you need (e.g., pod status or resource usage).

The best practice for writing a new record to Mongoose involves creating a new document and saving it to the database. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to do it:

1. Define your schema: Before you can create a new record, you need to define the schema for your model. Mongoose schemas define the structure of your documents.

Example:
```javascript
const mongoose = require('mongoose');

const schema = new mongoose.Schema({
  name: String,
  email: String,
  // Add more fields as needed
});
```
1. Create a model: Next, create a model using the schema.

Example:
```javascript
const User = mongoose.model('User', schema);
```
1. Create a new document: You can create a new document using the `new` keyword with the model and passing the data as properties of an object.

Example:
```javascript
const newUser = new User({
  name: 'John Doe',
  email: 'john.doe@example.com',
});
```
1. Save the document to the database: Use the `save()` method to store the new document in the database.

Example:
```javascript
newUser.save((error) => {
  if (error) {
    console.error(error);
  } else {
    console.log('User saved successfully');
  }
});
```
You can also use the `create()` method on the model as a shortcut for creating and saving a new document:
```javascript
User.create({
  name: 'John Doe',
  email: 'john.doe@example.com',
}, (error, createdUser) => {
  if (error) {
    console.error(error);
  } else {
    console.log('User created successfully');
    // Handle the created user
  }
});
```
Alternatively, you can use async/await syntax with `save()` or `create()`:
```javascript
try {
  const savedUser = await newUser.save();
  console.log('User saved successfully');
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error);
}

// Or using create():
try {
  const createdUser = await User.create({
    name: 'John Doe',
    email: 'john.doe@example.com',
  });
  console.log('User created successfully');
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error);
}
```
Remember to handle any errors that may occur during the process, as shown in the above examples.

