Here's a Perl script that checks if a given string is a well-formed XML document according to the XML 1.0 specification using the XML::LibXML module. This script handles documents with nested elements, attributes, namespaces, and CDATA sections and returns errors, if any.

To use this script, make sure to install the XML::LibXML module first.

You can install it using CPAN with the following command:

```bash
cpan install XML::LibXML
```

Then, you can use the following Perl script:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl

use strict;
use warnings;
use XML::LibXML;

# Sample XML string to test
my $xml_str = q{
<root>
   <book id="1">
     <title>Effective Perl Programming</title>
     <author>Joseph N.</author>
   </book>
   <book id="2">
     <title><![CDATA[Mastering Perl]]></title>
     <author>Brian D. Foy</author>
   </book>
   <book id="3" xmlns:pub="http://example.com/publications">
     <pub:title>Perl Best Practices</pub:title>
     <pub:author>Damian Conway</pub:author>
   </book>
</root>
};

sub is_valid_xml {
    my ($xml_data) = @_;
    my $parser = XML::LibXML->new;

    eval {
        $parser->parse_string($xml_data);
    };

    if ($@) {
        return (0, $@);
    } else {
        return (1, undef);
    }
}

my ($is_valid, $errors) = is_valid_xml($xml_str);

if ($is_valid) {
    print "The input is a valid XML document.\n";
} else {
    print "The input is NOT a valid XML document. Errors detected:\n$errors";
}
```

