To create dynamic filter expressions in Mapbox GL based on user input from a series of checkboxes, you can follow these steps:

1. Set up the basic Mapbox GL map with the desired data.
2. Create an HTML structure with checkboxes and link it to JavaScript using event listeners.
3. Update the filter expressions in your Mapbox GL layer based on the selected checkboxes.

Here's an example for your reference:

**HTML (index.html):**

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Mapbox GL</title>
  <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.6.1/mapbox-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.6.1/mapbox-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
</head>
<body style="margin:0;">
  <div id="map" style="position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;"></div>
  <div id="filters" style="position: absolute; top: 10px; left: 10px; background-color: white; padding: 10px">
    <input type="checkbox" id="option1" name="option1" value="option1">
    <label for="option1">Option 1</label><br>
    <input type="checkbox" id="option2" name="option2" value="option2">
    <label for="option2">Option 2</label><br>
    <input type="checkbox" id="option3" name="option3" value="option3">
    <label for="option3">Option 3</label><br>
  </div>
  <script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

**JavaScript (app.js):**

```
mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN';
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/light-v10',
  center: [-95.7129, 37.0902],
  zoom: 3
});

map.on('load', function () {
  // Add your data source and layer here
});

// Add event listeners for checkboxes
document.getElementById('option1').addEventListener('change', updateFilters);
document.getElementById('option2').addEventListener('change', updateFilters);
document.getElementById('option3').addEventListener('change', updateFilters);

function updateFilters() {
  const option1Checked = document.getElementById('option1').checked;
  const option2Checked = document.getElementById('option2').checked;
  const option3Checked = document.getElementById('option3').checked;

  let filters = ['any'];

  if (option1Checked) {
    filters.push(['==', 'property1', 'value1']);
  }
  if (option2Checked) {
    filters.push(['==', 'property2', 'value2']);
  }
  if (option3Checked) {
    filters.push(['==', 'property3', 'value3']);
  }

  // If no option is selected, display nothing
  if (filters.length === 1) filters = ['==', 'property', 'non-existent-value'];

  // Apply the new filter to the desired layer
  map.setFilter('your-layer-id', filters);
}
```

In the sample code above, replace `'your-layer-id'` with the ID of the desired layer with the filter applied. Make sure to use the appropriate property name and value in the filter expressions.

Now when you interact with the checkboxes, the filter expressions will be applied dynamically based on your selections.

To query data from an SQL database using JavaScript, you'll need to use a back-end Node.js server, such as Express, and an SQL library, like `mysql` or `pg`. I'm providing an example using Express and `mysql`. You can adapt this code to your preferred server and SQL library.

1. Install the required packages:

```bash
npm install express mysql
```

2. Create a file named `app.js` and add the following code:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const mysql = require('mysql');

const app = express();
app.use(express.static('public'));

// Your database connection details
const dbConfig = {
  host: 'localhost',
  user: 'your_user',
  password: 'your_password',
  database: 'your_database',
};

// Set up a route for fetching the data
app.get('/get-data', (req, res) => {
  const connection = mysql.createConnection(dbConfig);
  connection.connect();

  const query = `
    SELECT countries.name AS country, countries.capital as capital
    FROM countries, seas, countries_seas
    WHERE countries.id = countries_seas.country_id
    AND seas.id = countries_seas.sea_id
    AND seas.name = 'Mediterranean Sea';
  `;

  connection.query(query, (error, results, fields) => {
    if (error) {
      res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
      return;
    }

    res.json({ data: results });
  });

  connection.end();
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on http://localhost:3000'));
```

3. Set up a basic HTML file with a script for fetching the data and rendering it in a table:

```html
<!-- index.html -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Fetching Data Example</title>
</head>
<body>
  <table id="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Country</th>
        <th>Capital</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <script>
    fetch('/get-data')
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(json => {
        const table = document.getElementById('data-table');
        const tbody = table.querySelector('tbody');

        json.data.forEach(row => {
          const tr = document.createElement('tr');
          const tdCountry = document.createElement('td');
          const tdCapital = document.createElement('td');

          tdCountry.textContent = row.country;
          tdCapital.textContent = row.capital;
          tr.append(tdCountry, tdCapital);

          tbody.appendChild(tr);
        });
      })
      .catch(error => console.error('Error fetching data:', error));
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

4. Place the `index.html` file in the `public` directory.

5. Run the server:

```bash
node app.js
```

Now, if you access `http://localhost:3000` in your browser, you'll see the fetched data displayed in an HTML table format. Make sure to replace the `dbConfig` object properties with the correct values for your database.

