Yes, you can create a custom hook in React to check if an element is visible on the screen. You can use the intersection Observer API, which is available on modern browsers, to achieve this. The Intersection Observer API allows you to observe changes in the selection status of an element or an element's position in the DOM.

Here is a simple example of how you can create the custom hook:

```javascript
import { useCallback, useEffect, useRef } from 'react';

export function useIsElementVisible() {
  const ref = useRef(null);
  const handleIntersection = useCallback((entries) => {
    ref.current = entries[0];
    if (ref.current?.isIntersecting || ref.current?.intersectionRatio > 0) {
      // do something if the element is visible on screen
    } else {
      // do something if the element is not visible on screen
    }
  }, []);

  useEffect(() => {
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(handleIntersection);
    const targetElement = document.getElementById('your-target-element-id');
    observer.observe(targetElement, { threshold: 0.5 });
    return () => {
      observer.unobserve(targetElement);
    };
  }, []);
}

// Usage
export default function App() {
  useIsElementVisible();
  return (
    <div>
      <p>
        <a href="#" id="your-target-element-id">Visible Element</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        If the link is visible on screen, it will be highlighted.
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

This custom hook uses the useRef hook to maintain a reference to the target element, and a handleIntersection function to check if the element is visible using the Intersection Observer API. It then uses the useEffect hook to create and activate the observer, and clean it up when the component is unmounted. 

Please note that if your application lands on platforms such as Safari which doesn't support the Intersection Observer API, you will need to check for that case separately.

java.util.Date's getHours() method is deprecated because the Calendar class is recommended for date and time manipulation. A better approach is to use the java.time API classes, such as LocalDateTime, introduced in Java 8. Here's an example of refactoring code that uses the deprecated getHours() method:

Before (using the deprecated getHours() method):
```java
import java.util.Date;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Date date = new Date();
        int hour = date.getHours();
        System.out.println("Hour: " + hour);
    }
}
```

After (using the java.time API):
```java
import java.time.LocalDateTime;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDateTime now = LocalDateTime.now();
        int hour = now.getHour();
        System.out.println("Hour: " + hour);
    }
}
```

In the refactored version, we used the java.time.LocalDateTime class instead of java.util.Date to get the current hour. It is a much better practice to use the java.time API, as it is more modern and flexible than the older, deprecated methods.

