Sure, here is an example of an Nginx config that you can use as a reverse proxy for multiple services that use port 5050, 8080, and 4545:
```yaml
server {
    listen 80;
    listen [::]:80;
    server_name example.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:5050;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_connect_timeout 500;
        proxy_pass_request_headers On;
    }

    location /second-service {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:8080;
    }

    location /third-service {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:4545;
    }
}
```
In this config, we have three locations defined. Each location has a `proxy_pass` directive set to the corresponding service host and port. This makes Nginx act as a reverse proxy and forward the client's requests to the correct service.

Other important directives that are set in this config are:

* `proxy_http_version`: sets the HTTP version that Nginx should use when making the connection to the backend servers.
* `proxy_set_header`: sets additional headers that should be passed to the backend servers.
* `proxy_connect_timeout`: sets the timeout for connecting to the backend servers.
* `proxy_pass_request_headers`: specifies whether to pass the original client's headers to the backend servers.

If you have any further questions or need help customizing this config, please feel free to ask!

