MyDataObject data = new MyDataObject { Property1 = "value1", Property2 = "value2" };
string postResult = await restServiceWrapper.PostAsync("https://api.example.com/postResource", data);
```

Make sure to replace "your authToken here" with your actual authentication token, and update the URLs according to your API endpoints. Also, replace `MyDataObject` with the data object to be sent in the POST request.

An asynchronous counter is a digital counter where the output of one flip-flop in the counter chain drives the clock input of the next flip-flop. Here's a 4-bit asynchronous counter using Toggle flip-flops (T-flip flops) designed in VHDL:

```VHDL
library IEEE;
use IEEE.STD_LOGIC_1164.ALL;

entity async_4bit_counter is
    Port ( T : in  STD_LOGIC;
           CLK : in  STD_LOGIC;
           Q : out  STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (3 downto 0));
end async_4bit_counter;

architecture Behavioral of async_4bit_counter is
    signal temp : STD_LOGIC_VECTOR(3 downto 0);

    component T_FF is
    Port ( T : in  STD_LOGIC;
           CLK : in  STD_LOGIC;
           Q : out  STD_LOGIC;
           Q_bar : out  STD_LOGIC);
    end component;

begin
    Q <= temp;

    T_FF_0 : T_FF
    port map(
      T => T,
      CLK => CLK,
      Q => temp(0),
      Q_bar => open
    );

    T_FF_1 : T_FF
    port map(
      T => T,
      CLK => temp(0),
      Q => temp(1),
      Q_bar => open
    );

    T_FF_2 : T_FF
    port map(
      T => T,
      CLK => temp(1),
      Q => temp(2),
      Q_bar => open
    );

    T_FF_3 : T_FF
    port map(
      T => T,
      CLK => temp(2),
      Q => temp(3),
      Q_bar => open
    );
end Behavioral;
```

The counter consists of four T-flip flops connected in a chain. The output of one flip-flop is connected to the clock input of the next flip-flop, which results in an asynchronous (ripple) behavior. The output is a 4-bit binary count. Note that asynchronous counters can have issues with propagation delay in larger designs. In such cases, it's usually better to use a synchronous counter.

The `T_FF` component represents a T-flip flop and should be defined separately in a separate VHDL file. An example of a structural VHDL design for a T-flip flop using NAND gates can be found in many digital design resources.

To achieve this, you can use the useRef and useEffect hooks to get the width of the first div and set the width of the second div accordingly. Here's a simple example to help you understand this:

```
import React, { useRef, useState, useEffect } from "react";

const MyComponent = () => {
  const firstDivRef = useRef(null);
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const updateWidth = () => {
      if (firstDivRef.current) {
        setWidth(firstDivRef.current.offsetWidth);
      }
    };

    // Update width on initial render
    updateWidth();

    // Set up an event listener when the window is resized
    window.addEventListener("resize", updateWidth);

    // Clean up the event listener on unmounting
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener("resize", updateWidth);
    };
  }, []); // Empty dependency array to avoid unnecessary updates

  return (
    <>
      <div
        ref={firstDivRef}
        style={{ width: "50%", backgroundColor: "lightblue" }}
      >
        First div
      </div>
      <div
        style={{
          width: `${width}px`,
          backgroundColor: "lightgreen",
          marginTop: "10px",
        }}
      >
        Second div
      </div>
    </>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;
```

In this example, we create a reference for the first div and update the state variable 'width' whenever the window is resized. The width of the second div is set to the value of the 'width' state variable. This way, the second div's width always matches the first div's width, even when resizing the window.

