Here's the MATLAB script converted into a function named `buildSearchImageInfo`:
```matlab
function searchImageInfo = buildSearchImageInfo(imageFolder, channelToCorrelate)
    InfoStruct = dir(fullfile(imageFolder, "*.jpg"));
    searchImageInfo = struct2table(InfoStruct);
    searchImageInfo = convertvars(searchImageInfo, @iscell, 'string');
    searchImageInfo.Properties.VariableNames{'name'} = 'filename';

    name = extractBefore(searchImageInfo.filename, '.');
    searchImageInfo = addvars(searchImageInfo, name, 'Before', 1);

    imageDS = imageDatastore(fullfile(searchImageInfo.folder, searchImageInfo.filename));
    imageRGB = readall(imageDS);

    image = cellfun(@(x) x(:, :, channelToCorrelate), imageRGB, 'UniformOutput', false);
    searchImageInfo = addvars(searchImageInfo, image, imageRGB, 'After', 1);

    searchImageM = cellfun(@(x) size(x, 1), searchImageInfo.image);
    searchImageN = cellfun(@(x) size(x, 2), searchImageInfo.image);
    searchImageInfo = addvars(searchImageInfo, searchImageM, searchImageN, 'After', 2);
end
```
This function takes two input arguments: `imageFolder` and `channelToCorrelate`. You can call this function in your MATLAB script using:
```matlab
imageFolder = 'path_to_your_image_folder';
channelToCorrelate = 1; % for example, if you want to work with the first channel (Red in the RGB image)
searchImageInfo = buildSearchImageInfo(imageFolder, channelToCorrelate);
```
Replace `'path_to_your_image_folder'` with the actual path to the folder containing the images you want to process. The `channelToCorrelate` argument is the index of the channel you want to work with. For example, if you are working with RGB images and want to process the first channel (Red), set it to 1.

