Sure! You can use the following PowerShell code to enable Secure Boot on a Dell computer using WMI (Windows Management Instrumentation). Keep in mind that you need to run this script with Administrator privileges:

```powershell
# Get the WMI object for UEFI boot settings
$namespace = "root\dellomci"
$class = "DCIM_UEFIBootSetting"
$wmiObj = Get-WmiObject -Namespace $namespace -class $class

if ($wmiObj) {
    # Check if Secure Boot is already enabled
    if ($wmiObj.SecureBootStatus -eq "Enabled") {
        Write-Host "Secure Boot is already enabled."
    } else {
        try {
            # Enable Secure Boot (RequestMethod 3)
            $result = $wmiObj.InvokeMethod("RequestBoot", (3, "Enabled"))

            if ($result.ReturnValue -eq 0) {
                Write-Host "Secure Boot has been successfully enabled."
            } else {
                Write-Host "Failed to enable Secure Boot. Error code: $($result.ReturnValue)"
            }
        } catch {
            Write-Host "An error occurred while enabling Secure Boot: $_"
        }
    }
} else {
    Write-Host "Error: WMI object not found, please ensure the Dell OpenManage Client Instrumentation is installed."
}
```

This script queries the `DCIM_UEFIBootSetting` WMI class in the `root\dellomci` namespace to get the current Secure Boot status. If Secure Boot is not enabled, it uses the `RequestBoot` method to enable it. Note that this approach requires the Dell OpenManage Client Instrumentation software to be installed on the target system.

Additionally, be aware that enabling Secure Boot might cause boot issues with some older operating systems or unsigned drivers. Ensure that your system is compatible with Secure Boot before running this script.

